finding: src/cli/publish-fly.ts:188–199,287–307,447–453 — The shared check accepts a Machine with `config.auto_destroy: true` both before and after deploy. In a scratch copy, adding only that field to the matching Machine fixture at src/cli/publish-fly-target.node.ts:26 made the authorized-existing-app test still pass: the same auto-destroying Machine was returned by both lists and publish resolved. Fly's Machines API documents auto_destroy (https://machines-api-spec.fly.dev/models); stopping an auto-destroying Machine can destroy it instead of leaving it available for a cold start (https://community.fly.io/t/stopping-a-machine-via-the-cli-actually-destroys-it/10909). The attached volume can remain billable without a restartable Machine, contrary to stage 10's stop/start claim. No concurrent deploy or change after the inventory read is needed. This is an inventory acceptance reproduction, not a live reproduction of flyctl producing that state.
